<h2 id="why">Why it exists</h2>

A multi-agent process is a program. It has lifetimes, ownership, concurrency, cancellation, and failure, and it needs someone to be able to read it six weeks later. Frameworks that express the process as configuration or a graph DSL give up the one tool that already handles all of that well: a programming language.

Gimble keeps the process in Go and supplies only what Go does not have: agent sessions with owned lifetimes, context that reaches the agents in a scope, planner-directed loops, supervision of a live turn, and a record and a live view of all of it.

<h2 id="refuses">What it refuses to do</h2>

- **No named tactics.** There is no `BakeOff`, no `CritiqueRound`, no retry policy. Those are a few lines of Go in the workflow that needs them, where they can be read and changed.
- **No prompt assembly.** A prompt is a constant or an embedded file. Data reaches the agent as scoped context.
- **No models in workflows.** Workflows name roles. Runs bind them.
- **No agent runtime of its own.** Tools, sandboxes, and sign-in belong to the harness.
- **No hosted service.** The runtime, the record, and the console run on your machine, on loopback.

<h2 id="terms">Five terms</h2>

<dl>
  <dt>Run</dt><dd>One execution of a workflow and its record on disk.</dd>
  <dt>Scope</dt><dd>A named piece of a run. It ends when its function returns.</dd>
  <dt>Session</dt><dd>One agent conversation, on one harness, in one working directory.</dd>
  <dt>Role</dt><dd>A kind of cognitive work. A run binds each role to a harness, model, and effort.</dd>
  <dt>Group</dt><dd>A named set of concurrent child scopes. <code>Wait</code> joins them.</dd>
</dl>

<h2 id="attractor">Not an Attractor implementation</h2>

Gimble is a reaction to the work around Attractor, not an implementation of it. It does not adopt Attractor as its architecture, product plan, or vocabulary. The lesson kept is simple: keep the workflow in code a person can read, and make the agent work observable.

<h2 id="status">Status</h2>

Gimble is young and moves quickly. The Go API, the console, three harness adapters, and five built-in workflows exist today. Authoring a new CLI workflow currently happens inside the Gimble checkout; the library itself is usable from any Go module. The programming contract is the Godoc: `go doc -all github.com/tylergannon/gimble`.

</DocsPage>
